# XSH malloc()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Requests are small or moderate; failure is provoked only with a size no
# address space can hold. Pointers from a zero-size request may be null.

assertion  XSH.malloc.001
anchor     functions/malloc.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/malloc.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       malloc() of sizes from 1 byte to 1 MiB returns a non-null pointer to
           space of that many bytes, which can be written and read back.
test       t/001-usable.c

assertion  XSH.malloc.002
anchor     functions/malloc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Every pointer malloc() returns, whatever the size (1 byte up),
           is aligned for any basic type that fits in the requested size (so
           a multiple of the fundamental alignment once the block can hold the
           widest basic type), and objects of the widest basic types can be
           stored through it and read back.
test       t/002-aligned.c

assertion  XSH.malloc.003
anchor     functions/malloc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Sixty-four allocations of different sizes that are all live at once
           are pairwise disjoint: distinct fill patterns written into each
           remain intact in every other after all are written.
test       t/003-disjoint.c

assertion  XSH.malloc.004
anchor     functions/malloc.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       malloc(0) returns either a null pointer or a pointer that can be
           passed to free(); two such results that are both non-null and
           live at once differ.
test       t/004-zero-size.c

assertion  XSH.malloc.005
anchor     functions/malloc.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/malloc.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       When the space cannot be allocated (a request of SIZE_MAX bytes),
           malloc() returns a null pointer and sets errno to ENOMEM.
test       t/005-failure.c
